  "query_preview": ".. A strong monsoonal push is forecast to continue \nacross much of UT today as robust mid-level moisture returns to the \nregion. Cloud cover and leftover subsidence will once again keep \nmore widespread thunderstorm development delayed outside of a few \nweaker storms currently forming across southern UT. Convective \ninitiation is expected to occur around around 1300-1500, enabling \nthe environment to destabilize further with CAPE general ranging \nfrom 1000-1500J/kg statewide, though CAPE ~2000J/kg appears possible \ncloser to southwest UT. With PWATs generally 1.2\" or greater in \nplace across much of UT (0.8-1.0\" PWAT anomalies for this time of \nyear making their way across northern UT today as well), \nthunderstorms today are forecast to be efficient and heavy \nrainmakers. Mid-level flow is expected to weaken today, potentially \nresulting in showers and thunderstorms remaining anchored to \nterrain for most of the day. This results in some uncertainty for \npopulated areas along the northern UT I-15 corridor regarding if \nlower elevations will see any meaningful rainfall or not. \nAdditionally, this would pose a threat for storms remaining over \nproblematic terrain for an extended period of time, such as burn \nscars. \n\nThe Wasatch Back, Bear River Range, northern Uintas, and perhaps \nUinta county, WY may see particularly intense rain rates at times \ntoday as moisture continues to deepen across northern UT. Hi-res \nguidance has persistently shown a signal for rain rates potentially \nin excess of 1\"/hr at times. While these areas are historically less \nconcerning than southern UT regarding flash flooding concern, these \nrates are rather high for these areas and justified these areas \nbeing included within the flash flood watch this afternoon. \n\nFrom Thursday on, mid-level moisture will see a slight decrease \nareawide. However, with ridging remaining overhead, daily chances \nfor thunderstorms will continue across much of the state and favor \nterrain as steering flow aloft gradually weakens. It appears that \nglobal ensembles have begun to pick up on the monsoonal pattern \ncontinuing into next week, albeit less intense than this week. \n\nLastly, heat concerns begin to develop as ridging builds overhead \nresulting in widespread \"major\" HeatRisk developing across the \nWasatch Front starting Friday, expanding into central UT on \nSaturday. This threat is expected to continue through at least \nTuesday as overnight lows remain elevated in the mid to upper 70s, \noffering little relief from high daytime temperatures.",
